# Agent-facing Markdown (stay dry)

Write dry, reliable, instruction-dense prose. Optimize for correct agent behavior — not for being interesting to read. No marketing voice.

## Product facts (never violate)

- `mcpx` is a **CLI client**, not an MCP server/gateway. Never instruct agents to edit Cursor/IDE `mcp.json`.
- Tool commands always require explicit Server (`-s` / `--server`). No implicit default Server.

## Domain terms

Match `CONTEXT.md`: Server, Purpose, Tool, Config, Output, Agent Guide, Adapter, Transport.

## Preserve the contract

- Keep the three-step loop: list Servers → list Tools on one Server → call Tool.
- Keep Output conventions (JSON on stdout by default; errors on stderr / non-zero exit).
- Keep Config boundaries (`~/.mcpx/mcp.json`, `MCPX_CONFIG`; hand-edit / CLI manage — not IDE mcp.json).

## Adapters stay thin

Adapters (Cursor `.mdc`, `AGENTS.md` snippets) are pointers to the Agent Guide. Do not duplicate the full CLI reference or the whole Guide inside an Adapter.

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD: Adapter that re-documents every flag -->
## call-tool
Usage: mcpx call-tool --server NAME --tool TOOL --args '{...}'
(…full CLI dump…)

<!-- ✅ GOOD: thin pointer -->
Follow the Agent Guide at `agent-guide/AGENT_GUIDE.md`.
Always pass `-s` / `--server` on Tool commands.
```

## Diagrams

No decorative mermaid. Add a diagram only if it helps agents follow the three-step loop.
